brief:
Write the regression tests for contract E's two clauses, adding to the existing files and changing nothing already in them: a new TestCase class in test_record_paths.py with methods test_R8_..., and a new TestCase class in test_red_gate.py with methods test_G10_.... R8: exercise worker.is_record directly and tree_state() on a real temporary git repository with a tracked symlink committed at the declared directory's path and then deleted (and then replaced by a real directory with files), plus the no-tree-changes validator through worker.validate with worker.load_member("sibi"); skip (unittest.skip) only where the platform cannot make symlinks. G10: real temporary unittest files run through worker.unittest_red — body and subTest cases, not counter mocks. Before the build, record the reproduced baseline in your summary: the verdict worker.unittest_red returns today for the failureException = Exception + RuntimeError body file and for the subTest variant, and what tree_state() returns today for the deleted symlink. Every test that fails today must fail by an assertion, never by an error of the test itself. Import the candidate from this directory; no model, host or network calls; remove temporary directories. Run both files: the new tests must fail now by assertions only (zero errors) where the defect shows, and every earlier test in both files must still pass (35 in test_record_paths.py, 72 in test_red_gate.py). Do not change test_hacheong.py, test_native_prompt.py, test_hostcall_env.py or any non-test file.

CONTRACT E — two independent review findings, as two clauses. Everything earlier (R1-R7, G1-G9, N1-N7, H1-H6) stands unchanged; hostcall.py is frozen and is not touched.

R8 (directory boundary includes the directory itself). Found by independent review: worker.is_record(path, records) gives True for "reviews/a.json" with records ["reviews/"] but False for "reviews" itself, and git status names a declared directory's own path without a slash in real cases — a tracked symlink committed at "reviews" and then deleted is " D reviews", so tree_state() reports {"reviews": "gone"} although "reviews/" is a record. The rule, for worker.is_record(path, records) and so for every consumer (tree_state, both inventories):
- an entry ending in "/" covers the directory's own path (the entry without its trailing slash, e.g. "reviews") and every path under it ("reviews/a.json", "reviews/x/y.json"); never a sibling sharing a prefix ("reviews-old", "reviews-old/a.json", "reviews.py", "reviewsx"); root-anchored, so "src/reviews" and "src/reviews/a.json" are not covered; a nested declared directory follows the same rule ("docs/rec/" covers "docs/rec" and "docs/rec/x.json", not "docs/record", "docs/rec.md" nor "x/docs/rec");
- an entry without a trailing "/" stays an exact file: it covers exactly that path — never a child ("hunsu.json/x"), a prefix neighbour ("hunsu.json.bak") or anything else, and no filesystem stat or type check turns it into a directory (a bare record "mangsang" does not cover "mangsang/x.json" even when a directory "mangsang" exists on disk);
- tree_state(): after a tracked symlink at a declared directory record's path (e.g. "reviews" with "reviews/" declared, or with no lock, where "reviews/" is a fallback record) is deleted — and also when a real directory with files then takes its place — neither "reviews" nor anything under it appears in the state; a tracked symlink deleted at an undeclared path ("reviews-old") still appears as "gone"; and the no-tree-changes validator does not fail a call whose only change is that deleted record symlink.

G10 (only AssertionError failures are assertions). Found by independent review: a real unittest file whose TestCase sets `failureException = Exception` and whose test body does `raise RuntimeError('runtime failure, not assertion')` makes worker.unittest_red return ("red", "unittest recorded an assertion failure inside a test body"): the harness trusts unittest's addFailure / test.failureException and never checks that the exception is an AssertionError. A failing subTest under such a broad failureException does the same. The accepted contract says a non-assertion runtime error does not prove red. So:
- in REPORT, a failure (addFailure, or addSubTest with an exception unittest treats as a failure) counts in "assertion_failures" or "fixture_failures" only when its exception type is AssertionError or a subclass of it; any other failure — however the TestCase's failureException is set — counts in "errors" (so red_verdict gives "unverified"); fail closed for any failure type that cannot be established as an AssertionError;
- on real temporary files, unittest_red gives "unverified" for: failureException = Exception with a body raising RuntimeError; the same with the RuntimeError inside a subTest; failureException = Exception with self.fail(...) in the body (self.fail then raises a plain Exception); and a file with one genuine assertion failure plus one such non-AssertionError failure;
- it still gives "red" for a genuine AssertionError (self.fail / assertEqual / bare assert) in a test body, also when the TestCase sets failureException to a subclass of AssertionError and the body fails through it, and also for a bare `assert` in a TestCase whose failureException = Exception; assertion failures in setUp/tearDown still give "unverified"; every G1-G9 verdict stays as it is.
